# Answer 1 · The Status of Women's Studies in Pakistan and the Autonomy/Integration Debate (CSS 2016)
@py question 2016-2

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Two limbs: a note on the status of the field, then your views on the debate — argue for a dual strategy.
@ask ① WRITE A NOTE on the status of Women's Studies in Pakistan
@ask ② GIVE YOUR VIEWS on the autonomy/integration debate
(1) Introduction: define WS; 1989; thesis — young field, dual strategy
(2) Origins: NGOs 1975–86; WAF 1981; Mumtaz and Shaheed 1987
(3) Institutional status: five centres 1989; degrees; journals
(4) Achievements: research; professionals; policy; laws
(5) Problems: funding; faculty; curriculum; resistance; ghetto
(6) The debate explained: Bowles and Klein 1983; autonomy; integration
(7) Case for each and risks: depth vs reach; ghetto vs dilution
(8) My view for Pakistan: dual strategy; integrate law, medicine, economics
(9) Conclusion: field has arrived; needs roots and reach
:::

## Decoding the question
Two command phrases, two limbs. **"Write a note on the status"** asks for a structured description of where the field stands today — its history, institutions, achievements and problems — not a history of feminism. **"Give your views"** asks for a clear personal position on the autonomy/integration debate, argued with evidence. The examiner wants you to *join* the limbs: your view on the debate should grow out of the problems you identify in the first limb.

## The model answer

### (1) Introduction
**Women's Studies** is the academic field that makes women the subject, the knowers and the beneficiaries of research; it began in American universities around ==1969–70== as "the academic arm of the women's movement". In Pakistan it was institutionalised in ==1989==, when the federal Ministry of Women's Development created Women's Studies centres in five universities. Three decades later the field is recognised and productive, but still fragile. Its future depends on combining an autonomous academic base with the integration of gender into every discipline — the "dual strategy".

### (2) The origins of the field in Pakistan
Research on women began outside the universities. **Shirkat Gah** (==1975==) and the **Aurat Foundation** (==1986==) documented women's legal status, work and political participation. The **Women's Action Forum** (==1981==), formed to resist the Hudood Ordinances of ==1979==, produced legal and social analysis, and Khawar Mumtaz and Farida Shaheed's *Women of Pakistan: Two Steps Forward, One Step Back?* (==1987==) gave the country its first standard history of the women's movement. The universities followed the activists.

### (3) The institutional status today
- **The centres of 1989:** Quaid-i-Azam University (Islamabad), the Universities of Karachi, the Punjab (Lahore), Peshawar and Balochistan (Quetta). Some were later upgraded to centres of excellence, and most have renamed themselves "Gender Studies".
- **Degrees:** BS, MPhil and PhD programmes are now offered at many public universities, and gender courses appear in sociology, social work and development programmes.
- **Publications:** journals such as the *Pakistan Journal of Gender Studies* and *Alam-e-Niswan* publish local research.
- **Recognition:** from ==CE-2016== Gender Studies became a CSS optional subject.

The field has also broadened its subject. The early centres concentrated on women's legal status and violence; today theses and projects cover masculinities, climate and disaster, the informal economy, digital harassment, disability and the lives of minority women — a sign that the field is moving from Women's Studies towards a fuller Gender Studies.

### (4) Achievements
1. **Research** on honour crimes, domestic violence, family law, political participation and women's work that fed the protective laws of 2006–2016.
2. **Trained professionals** who staff the National Commission on the Status of Women (==2000==), provincial commissions, women's protection centres and NGOs.
3. **Policy influence**, for example on the National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women (==2002==) and the Gender Reform Action Plan of the early 2000s.
4. **A public vocabulary** — harassment, domestic violence, care work — for problems once treated as private.

These achievements matter because they show the field working as intended: research moving from the university into law. When Parliament debated the workplace-harassment law of ==2010== and the anti-honour-killing amendment of ==2016==, the evidence on which legislators and campaigners relied had been built up over two decades by researchers, many of them trained in Women's Studies. The field's influence is often invisible, but it is real.

### (5) Problems
::: table | The field's problems and their effects.
@widths 3,7
Problem | Effect
Donor-dependent funding | Centres shrink when projects end; agendas set from outside
Shortage of specialist faculty | Few PhDs; heavy reliance on teachers from other disciplines
Western-derived curriculum, mostly in English | Weak local theory; limited reach beyond elite students
Weak research base | Small samples; little long-term data on Pakistani families
Social resistance | Seen as a "Western" or "anti-family" agenda
Ghettoisation | Mostly women students and staff; little influence on other departments
:::

Two of these problems deserve emphasis. **Funding** shapes everything else: when a donor project ends, research teams scatter, and the next project brings a new agenda set in Islamabad or abroad rather than by Pakistani scholars. **Resistance** is equally serious: because the field questions family roles, it is easily portrayed as foreign and hostile to religion. The answer to both is the same — secure public funding through the Higher Education Commission and research that is visibly rooted in Pakistan's own society.

### (6) The autonomy/integration debate explained
The debate was framed by **Gloria Bowles and Renate Duelli Klein** in *Theories of Women's Studies* (==1983==). **Autonomy** means a separate department with its own staff, degrees, budget and research agenda. **Integration** means weaving women and gender into the content of every existing discipline — history, economics, law, medicine.

### (7) The case for each — and the risks
Autonomy gives the field **space, depth and a critical voice**: new theory needs a home, and a department signals legitimacy. Integration gives **reach and transformation**: every future doctor, lawyer and economist meets gender, and the disciplines themselves are corrected. Each has a danger. Autonomy risks **ghettoisation** — a small "women's corner" that the rest of the university ignores. Integration risks **dilution** — "add a lecture and stir", dependent on sympathetic individuals and easily dropped.

### (8) My view: a dual strategy for Pakistan
Pakistan cannot choose one side, because its problems demand both. The field is young and short of trained faculty, so it needs **strong autonomous departments** to produce specialists and local research. But the greatest impact lies in **integration**: maternal health in medical colleges, family and criminal law in law schools, unpaid work in economics, gender-sensitive textbooks in teacher training, and gender analysis in civil-service academies.

::: balance | The dual strategy in one line
Autonomy is the engine; integration is the road. An autonomous centre should train teachers for other departments, co-teach their courses and supply their research — as the Beijing Platform (==1995==) later combined specific measures with gender mainstreaming.
:::

The counter-argument deserves a fair hearing: some scholars fear that in a conservative academic environment, integration will be token — a single lecture on "women" in a law or medical course, taught by someone with no training. That risk is real, which is why integration must be *driven by* the autonomous departments, with trained teachers and approved course content, rather than left to chance. Such a strategy also answers the charge of a "Western agenda": integration into local disciplines, research on Pakistani families and publication in Urdu root the field in Pakistan's own society.

### (9) Conclusion
Women's Studies in Pakistan has moved in three decades from activist research to recognised academic discipline, with real influence on law and policy. Yet it remains dependent on donors, short of faculty and confined to a corner of the university. The autonomy/integration debate is therefore not academic: it is the choice between a field that stays small and one that changes how Pakistan thinks. The answer is both — a well-funded autonomous base that makes gender part of every discipline.

## Why this answer scores
- Both limbs are answered, under headings taken from the question's words ("status"; "your views").
- The status is evidenced — dates, institutions, journals, laws, the NCSW — not asserted.
- The problems table gives the examiner the whole diagnosis at a glance.
- The debate is attributed to its source (Bowles and Klein, 1983), which is on the reading list.
- The view is clear, argued and applied to Pakistan, and links the two limbs: the problems of limb 1 justify the dual strategy of limb 2.
